  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe convectively-reinforced cold front has since moved south of the \nCWA, with low-level stratus advecting over the region. GOES-East \nvisible imagery has detected the typical waveform structure to the \npost-frontal stratus deck, which will act to perturb the overturned \nairmass enough such that elevated showers and perhaps a few storms \nmay develop this afternoon given the presence of the now-depleted \nEML. Severe weather nor flash flooding are expected with afternoon \nconvection, and updrafts may be too shallow to reach heights high \nenough to generate lightning. PoPs have been delineated along a line \nfrom HTS-CRW-CKB, with the best chances for afternoon showers and \nstorms along and south-to-southeast of that line. The potential for \nshowers and storms will wane quickly by sunset this evening as the \nboundary-layer decouples, with valley fog expected to develop late \ntonight into Saturday morning across most of the CWA. Visibilities \nless than one-quarter of a mile will occur across some locales, \nalong with the cloud deck descending to 100-200 ft AGL, with light, \nnortherly winds becoming calm and easterly overnight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nFog and low stratus will begin to lift by the mid-morning hours \nSaturday as boundary-layer mixing commences. Entrainment of drier \nair in the mid-levels will allow stronger insolation to occur, \nboosting highs into the middle-upper 80s across the lowlands while \ncooler in the mountains. Dry weather is expected during the day \nSaturday given the strong warm nose/subsidence near 800 mb, but as \nthe subtropical ridge continues to retrograde westward into the \nsouthern Great Plains, it will begin to flatten owing to the \nejection of a neutrally-tilted shortwave trough over the central \nRocky Mountains. The resultant interaction of these synoptic-scale \nfeatures will allow a PV anomaly to shear off of an intense \nshortwave trough digging into Ontario, resulting in a belt of moist, \nisentropic ascent to advect across the Rust Belt and towards the \nnorthwestern portions of the CWA late Saturday night. The arrival of \nthe leading shortwave perturbation will spawn a surface low in the \nGreat Lakes region, with the cold front that moved through today \nundergoing warm-frontogenesis and lifting northeastward across the \nCWA. The subtropical ridge will lose its influence over the region \nby the latter half of the weekend as the aforementioned shortwave \ntrough over the central Rocky Mountains progresses into the OH River \nValley while broadening in amplitude. The presence of the increasing \ncyclonic flow aloft, which is forecast to be accompanied by strong \njetlets in the mid/upper-levels, will provide large-scale forcing \nfor ascent and daily chances for heavy showers and storms next week.",
